@Long time paid subscriber
I'm sorry, but I believe there are occasions for harmless usage of "lame," and in fact it's not particularly offensive (not even a little, really) as applies to a disability.  "Gimpy" would come across as tacky, but "lame" is just rather out-dated. ...and I am speaking as one with a close family member who has had a disability that prevents her from having a normal gait for...oh, about 45 years now.  
Now, I do use "lame" when referring to our state's Senators, but that's because I don't want to use really bad words.

@Long time paid subscriber
Thank you for saying this, I came here to write something similar. This was an enjoyable if tricky crossword, but I hope the editing staff might consider how hurtful it can be to have the answer to the clue "Uncool" be a word meaning "disabled". This is actually the second time in a month that they've had this clue/answer pair and it bothered me both times. 

If those letters must be used, why not reuse a different pattern and ask for an answer in French: lamé or l'âme. Or, maybe just make the clue "Unable to move or walk easily". There are ways to fit a crossword without reinforcing an association between injury/disability and "uncool". Intentional or not, our use of language matters, and I hope those of us who enjoy lighthearted wordplay will consider that.

