@Steve L
I have now decided that there is no such thing as trivia. Every bit of information is useful or meaningful or cherished by someone. Maybe not by you or your group (however you define them) but to someone else. I've decided that, since I haven't run out of brain space so far, I'll just keep cramming bits of stuff in there. Locating what I want may be harder but I still get the "aha!" when I see the answer even if I couldn't snatch it from the whirling brainstorm. So I'm still interested to know who won the 1982 Super Bowl; which end of the battery is the anode and which, the diode; why daytime clouds are sometimes grey and sometimes white; whether "rose" began as a more general word for flower and then became narrower, or vice versa; and on and on.
I want to know as much as I can possibly know.

@HeathieJ, I'm very sorry for your loss. It's too soon and unfair. 💔

I agree with your views on puzzles and attitudes/opinions on what should fly and what is unacceptable. Aside from the fact that many have their own, often erroneous, views on the rules of crosswords, I just feel that if a puzzle isn't in your wheelhouse you can politely say so and move on. Denigrating constructors and generally complaining about what you didn't like serves no real purpose.

Do I like every puzzle I solve? Nope. But I can't imagine disliking a puzzle to a degree of ranting about it online.

